Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["7. Analytical Discussion and Quantitative Insights", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"ad5c5460-5900-4ea1-aced-f507520428d2"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
(A) Why ARMA Models Excel at Expected Average Price Prediction\
\>", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"4bb1f5d0-d434-4d80-aad6-d02607e7d34d"],

Cell["\<\
1. Mean Reversion to Unconditional Drift: In liquid equities, daily returns \
mean-revert rapidly to the long-term equity risk premium drift (mu_r approx \
+0.09%/day for SPY). ARMA forecasts converge to this drift, providing a \
grounded multi-step price expectation that does not explode.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d3a26402-9995-4a7b-b327-787cb20fbc6a"],

Cell["\<\
2. Noise Absorption: Transitory daily noise shocks are absorbed by the moving \
average (MA) polynomial, preventing transient market volatility from \
distorting multi-week valuation expectations.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"559956cd-e0d3-461e-86ce-fa849f8971d6"],

Cell["\<\
3. Across 16 rolling windows, ARMA achieved a 1.64% MAPE on average future \
price, outperforming both the Naive benchmark (2.10%) and the pure linear \
Kalman trend (2.62%). \
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"21a5655a-f2ef-4df0-b632-a6054fc13927"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["(B) The Strengths and Limitations of the Kalman Filter", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"6e4d6b93-ff03-4e8f-afa5-63b56e830de9"],

Cell["\<\
1. Zero Phase Lag in Dynamic Trend Extraction: Unlike simple moving averages \
(which lag price changes by half their window width, e.g. ~10 days for a \
20-day SMA), the Kalman filter level mu_t dynamically tracks true shifts with \
minimal delay while attenuating high-frequency noise.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"08f34a00-7333-46bc-bc30-3045519ab903"],

Cell["\<\
2. Momentum Quantification: The slope state beta_t ($/day) provides an \
instantaneous measure of trend direction and acceleration.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"57463b2c-4fea-462d-ab0c-876beb9914a7"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "3. Extrapolation Risk (Momentum Overshoot): Linear extrapolation (P",
 StyleBox["{T+h} = mu",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 "T + h * beta_T) assumes uninhibited momentum continuation. If a stock \
enters the forecast window on a sharp short-term rally, the Kalman filter \
projects continued linear growth, resulting in overshooting if the market \
consolidates or pulls back. "
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"97e17dfc-d646-4959-9cda-115d06d4d188"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["(C) Quantitative Finance Architecture Recommendation", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"54e3c249-0b97-4eba-bbf5-3417bba9a2c4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "1. In-Sample Signal Generation: Use the Kalman Filter to extract zero-lag \
trends (mu",
 StyleBox["t) and instantaneous momentum (beta",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 "t) for regime detection and trade entry/exit triggers."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"9f3631b3-57b3-4e85-9b0d-852b140d0652"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "2. Forward Valuation & Horizon Expectation: Use ARMA on returns (or a \
Damped-Trend Kalman Filter where beta",
 StyleBox["{t+1} = phi * beta",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 "t with 0 < phi < 1) when computing expected average future prices, forward \
strike targets, or risk limits over multi-week horizons."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"fd681308-47c3-4feb-8d9c-e5bc10c89a81"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]]
